Course 3: Passing accuracy (Detailed Research)

Category: Technique
Duration: 20 min
Level: 2/5
What the Course Should Cover

This course focuses on short and long passes aiming for accuracy and rhythm, including passing under pressure. The modern game requires players to progress the ball efficiently through precise, well-weighted passes while under defensive pressure. FIFA's John Peacock emphasizes that "small details can make a big difference in determining how successfully a team will be able to progress the ball. Subtle movements and body positioning can allow players to receive the ball with greater control and make more progressive passes".
Key Coaching Points
Body Positioning Before Receiving

    Open up your body and play on the half-turn to give yourself multiple passing options and progress the ball more effectively

    Adopt a side-on, open body position and be ready to receive the ball on the half-turn to evade pressing players

    Scan for 'BOTS' before receiving: Ball position, Opposition players, Teammates, and Space

    Orientate the body so that it can open in the direction the player wishes to go as they receive

First Touch

    A quality first touch away from oncoming opponents allows players to keep the ball and play into space

    Focus on a firm and close first touch that will prevent the ball from getting caught under foot

    A precise first touch allows players to get the ball out of their feet and play in the direction they wish to pass

    Control the ball on the half-turn to receive in a position to progress play

Passing Technique

    Play firm, accurate passes to enhance the fluidity of the circuit

    Accurately weight the passes so that they are not played too firmly, making them difficult to control, or too softly, making them easy to intercept

    Well-weighted and well-timed passes help prevent heavy touches or interceptions, allowing players to play quickly and keep possession

    Firm, incisive and well-weighted passes can allow a team to progress the ball more effectively and avoid oncoming opponents

    Pass to the correct foot of your teammate (away from pressure)

Movement Off the Ball

    Effective movement off the ball can provide multiple passing lanes for team-mates to play into

    Players without the ball should always create lines of passes to give options to teammates and create spaces

    Rotate positions so that there is less predictability for defenders to read and more space can be opened up

    Time your movement into space to receive the ball away from immediate pressure

Common Mistakes to Address
Mistake	Why It Happens	Correction
Pass is intercepted	Poor weight/power on pass	"Play firm, accurate passes" - not too hard or too soft
Player needs extra touch to control	First touch is too heavy or behind player	"Take a positive first touch away from pressure" and "get the ball out of feet"
Player gets caught in possession	Receiving facing own goal or closed body	"Open up body and play on the half-turn"
Passing lane disappears	Teammate is static	"Create passing lanes" and "constant movement off the ball"
Pass goes behind teammate	Not passing to correct foot	"Pass to the correct foot of your teammate (away from pressure)"
Sample Drills
Drill 1: Two-Player Wall Passes (Warm-up) — 5 minutes

    Players work in pairs, 5-8 yards apart

    Pass back and forth using inside of foot

    After 10-15 passes, switch to weaker foot

    Progressions: One-touch passing, directional first touch, increasing distance

Coaching points:

    Plant foot next to the ball

    Lock the ankle

    Firm, accurate passes along the ground

Drill 2: Triangle Passing with Movement — 5 minutes

    Three cones in a triangle (6-10 yards per side)

    One player at each cone, one ball

    Player A passes to B and moves to B's place

    Player B receives, opens up, passes to C, then moves

    Keep the triangle rotation going

Progressions: Limit to two touches, add second ball, change direction

Coaching points:

    First touch into space, away from the cone

    Body open to see the next pass

    Timing of movement after each pass

Drill 3: Passing Gates for Precision — 5 minutes

    Place multiple small gates (two cones 1-2 yards apart) across the field

    Players work in pairs, 5-10 yards from a gate

    Pass the ball through the gate to each other

    After 10 successful passes, move to another gate or increase distance

Progressions: Weaker foot only, one-touch passes, timed challenges
Drill 4: One-Touch Rondo (4v1 or 5v2) — 5 minutes

    Square or circle grid, 8-12 yards

    Four or five attackers on the outside, one or two defenders in the middle

    Outside players keep possession, passing around defenders

    Start with two touches, then progress to one-touch

Coaching points:

    Play with your head up

    Use the correct surface of the foot

    Pass to the correct foot of your teammate (away from pressure)

    Encourage constant movement and support angles

Progression Variables

    Touch limit: Two touches → one touch → unlimited but quick

    Space: Larger area → smaller area to increase decision speed

    Pressure: No defender → passive defender → active defender

    Passing distance: Short passes → mixed short and long → long passes

    Direction demand: Free passing → directional passing → passing to progress play

Assessment Indicators

A player has improved when they:

    Weights passes correctly so teammates can control with one touch

    Scans before receiving (checking shoulders for BOTS)

    Receives on the half-turn and can play forward immediately

    Creates passing lanes through intelligent movement off the ball

    Maintains possession under pressure through firm, accurate passing

